  "actual_commencement_date": null,
  "description": "Replacement of single-glazed windows with double-glazed windows for reasons of insulation and effecting proper repairs.\n\nThis work was done in December 2024 on the understanding that this was permitted development, but in view of the Article 4 direction pertaining to the conservation area CA24, retrospective permission is now being sought.\n\nThe affected windows are:\n\n1. the ground floor bay window on the front elevation (house window 1)\n2. 2 windows on the first floor rear elevation facing north (house windows 2 and 3)\n3. 1 window on the side elevation facing east at first floor level (house window 4)\n4. 2 small cellar windows on the side elevation facing east at pathway level (cellar 1 and cellar 2).\n\nIn the case of all of the house windows, they were wood originally and have been replaced as wood. There has been no change to the frames or box surround either internally or externally, and only the sliding panes/ sashes have been replaced like for like.\n\nIn the case of the cellar windows, cellar 1 was single glazed, wood framed and covered with a grille, and cellar 2 had a wooden frame but no glazing, and it was boarded instead with an extraction fan inserted. Due to the position of these 2 windows just above pathway level, and to the fact they are invisible from the street, behind a high gate (even when this is open), uPVC was used. \n\nThe location of the windows are marked on the property floorpan and the cellar windows are additionally shown on a marked-up version of the site plan. \n\nEach of the windows have \"before\" and \"after\" photographs.",
